---
pagination:
  data: trips
  size: 1
  alias: trip
permalink: "/trips/{{ trip.tripId | tripSlug }}/"
layout: layout.njk
styles: ["/css/arrivals.css", "/css/trip.css"]
scripts: ["/js/back.js", "/js/trip.js"]
eleventyComputed:
  title: "{{ trip.route.routeShortName }} to {{ trip.headsign }}"
  description: "Every stop on the {{ trip.route.routeShortName }} to {{ trip.headsign }}, leaving at {{ trip.startTime | clock }}"
---
{% from "icon.njk" import icon with context %}
{#
  One page per trip: where a bus goes after it leaves you, and when it gets
  there. Reached by tapping an arrival on a stop page, which passes the stop it
  came from in `?from=` so /js/trip.js can point out the rider's own row.

  The list is rendered by /js/render-trip.js through the `tripCalls` filter,
  not by a loop here — the same module will redraw it in the browser with live
  times in it, and two implementations of one list is how they drift apart.

  Unlike a stop page this needs no filling in after the build: a timetable is
  the same on every day the trip runs, which is why there is one page rather
  than one per day.
#}
<main class="container container-md">
  <header class="surface trip-header">
    <button type="button" class="icon-btn" data-back aria-label="Go back" title="Go back">
      {{ icon("arrowBack") }}
    </button>

    <div class="trip-header-body">
      <h1 class="trip-name">
        <span
          class="pill"
          style="background-color: {{ trip.route.routeColor }}; color: {{ trip.route.routeTextColor | default(trip.route.routeColor | contrastText, true) }}; border-color: {{ 'currentColor' if trip.route.routeColor | tooLight else 'transparent' }}"
          >{{ trip.route.routeShortName }}</span
        >
        <span class="trip-headsign">to {{ trip.headsign }}</span>
      </h1>
      <p class="trip-meta">
        {{ trip.calls.length }} stops · {{ trip.startTime | clock }} – {{ trip.endTime | clock }}{% if trip.runs %} · {{ trip.runs }}{% endif %}
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  {#
    The route's colour reaches every dot on the rail from here. The two flags
    are the ends it can fail at — a colour too pale to see on the light theme,
    or too dark to see on the dark one — and the stylesheet falls back to the
    border grey for either.
  #}
  <div
    class="trip-calls-block"
    id="trip-calls"
    data-trip-id="{{ trip.tripId }}"
    data-too-light="{{ trip.route.routeColor | tooLight }}"
    data-too-dark="{{ trip.route.routeColor | tooDark }}"
    style="--route-color: {{ trip.route.routeColor }}"
  >
{#-
  On one line, with no whitespace inside the markers: the worker rewrites
  exactly what is between them, so a page it writes and a page the build wrote
  have to be able to come out byte for byte the same. Otherwise every trip page
  looks out of date to the worker the moment it starts.
-#}
<!--calls:start-->{{ trip.calls | tripCalls | safe }}<!--calls:end-->
  </div>

  {#
    The way back to the arrivals the reader came from, at the end of a list
    thirty-odd stops long. /js/trip.js fills in the link and reveals it when the
    page was opened from a stop; there is nowhere to go back to otherwise, so it
    ships hidden.
  #}
  <div class="trip-switch">
    <a class="btn btn-primary" data-back-to-stop hidden></a>
    <a class="btn btn-outline" href="/">Find a Stop</a>
  </div>
</main>
